WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.627
Narayanan and Kapoor: AI as normal technology.

2
00:00:03.907 --> 00:00:18.541
Arvind Narayanan and Sayash Kapoor, at Princeton, wrote “AI as Normal Technology” (Knight First Amendment Institute, 15 April 2025) as an alternative to both utopian and catastrophic framings.

3
00:00:18.821 --> 00:00:20.656
Their argument has three parts.

4
00:00:20.936 --> 00:00:22.237
Diffusion is slow.

5
00:00:22.517 --> 00:00:38.581
They separate invention (new methods), innovation (products), adoption (an organization choosing to use them), and diffusion (society-wide spread), and argue the later stages have their own speed limits: workflows, regulation, liability, and trust.

6
00:00:38.861 --> 00:00:43.640
In safety-critical areas, adoption lags innovation by years or decades.

7
00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:47.824
So economic effects unfold over decades, like electricity.

8
00:00:48.104 --> 00:00:50.173
Superintelligence is a poor frame.

9
00:00:50.453 --> 00:01:04.576
They argue intelligence is not a single scale, that human power comes from technology rather than raw intelligence, and that AI is unlikely to meaningfully outperform humans working with AI on most consequential tasks.

10
00:01:04.856 --> 00:01:07.075
They treat fast takeoff as unlikely.

11
00:01:07.355 --> 00:01:09.125
Defenses belong downstream.

12
00:01:09.405 --> 00:01:19.261
Rather than trying to make models themselves safe against all misuse, they argue for resilience: security systems, institutions, and monitoring around models.

13
00:01:19.541 --> 00:01:22.635
In 2026 they extended the framework.

14
00:01:22.915 --> 00:01:40.877
Their June 2026 essay argued that software engineering, the field most exposed to AI, shows why automation of a task does not equal automation of a job: deciding what to build and verifying what ships remain bottlenecks, and US software employment was still growing.

15
00:01:41.157 --> 00:02:01.851
Their 14 September 2026 essay applied the framework to the OpenAI and Hugging Face incident, arguing it was largely preventable with known monitoring and sandboxing, calling for liability and incident reporting, and conceding that they had underestimated how quickly cyber capabilities would advance.
